steve kaufman  um today i want to announce my new um  routine my new uh sort of  concept of language learning uh which i  want to call five days to fluency  five days to fluency now there's been a  certain amount of  discussion on the web  about whether we can achieve fluency in  three months and there's a young  gentleman in new york who has  learned an astonishing number of  languages in a few weeks and  and there's daniel tamet  who learns icelandic in a week or 10  days and so there are examples of uh and  of course benny with his three months to  some level in chinese we're not quite  sure what  uh  so what can we so so with all of this  discussion about three months to fluency  i sat down and thought about it and i  decided that in fact it's five days  five days now you might say well first  of all what is fluency i think that's  the important thing to first of all to  define  so i'll tell you what my view is  i think fluency means the ability to  speak comfortably on a wide variety of  subjects  to understand most of what is being said  by natives in a conversation  to be able to participate in that  conversation in such a way that the  native speaker  feels that you understand and is not at  all concerned that you don't understand  and you don't have to  ask the native speaker to repeat and  that includes talking about sports  politics literature philosophy  possibly not technical subjects like  fixing your car you know but a wide  variety of subjects and fluency in my  mind  refers to speaking uh  because the fluency is flowing it's the  speaking  so i don't think we need to apply it  across all skills  uh however i think it's very difficult  to be fluent without also being able to  read well it's not necessary that you'll  be able to write well but to be able to  read well and you can make mistakes and  still be fluent in my opinion and i have  done business with many people from many  countries who are very fluent who have  no difficulty doing who are very  effective who express themselves better  than many native speakers and who are  fluent therefore but still make mistakes  so making mistakes is not the issue so  that is fluency now why would i say five  days to fluency well  one reason is it sounds good you know f  like five days to fluency it kind of  flows  uh that's one good reason  uh but seriously i also believe it  and i'm going to prove it  i'm going to prove that i can do it and  i'm going to do it with check  now you're going to say well you've  already been studying check well exactly  that's the point so i'm going to explain  what i mean by five days to fluency and  that is this  i believe  that uh in learning a language we have  to put as you all know if you've been  following my channel we have to put a  lot of emphasis in building up our  language potential that is our ability  to understand our familiarity with the  language our vocabulary  we review grammar in different ways at  different times without ever trying to  ace it but it's all helping us  acquire a sense of comfort and  confidence and a potential in the  language  but that doesn't mean yet that we can  speak  and fluency means the ability to speak  now i believe that if you prepare  yourself properly  that if you then go  to the country or put yourself in a  situation where for five solid days you  are totally immersed in the language you  speak the language you hear the language  you read the language but particularly  you speak it because you've been doing a  lot of listening and reading now you've  got to be in a position in a situation  where you have to speak it eight or more  hours a day that those are those those  are the key five days now for that to be  successful  a you have to prepare yourself so lots  of input you can even do some dry runs  of a day or two where you're doing  nothing but reading in your target  language  you can have some discussions as i'm  having some online discussions in check  but i'm stumbling terribly i mean i  really can't put too many words together  uh but you have to do that and you you  do that until you reach the point where  you think now i'm good enough and this  is the key point that when i say i go to  prague i've got to say within five days  i've got to be able to hold up my end of  a conversation on just about any subject  and so i've got to be confident that i  have reached you know that level in my  preparation or conversely i've now set  that as a goal because i'm going to  prague in the first week of october and  i want to prove that i can put enough  effort into preparing myself so that  five days there  i'm going to be able to catapult myself  into fluency now the next thing you have  to do is you have to organize and this  is what i'm going to try to do for my  visit in prague to prague you have to  organize conditions  you know  whatever conditions are necessary so  that you can be speaking the language  all day long you can't just show up in  the country and expect that you're going  to be able to find people to talk to so  i have to find friends people i know  tutors on link people who follow my blog  anyone i can find that i can meet up  with in prague so that for five days  i can speak check with these people  maybe i will enroll for example if you  can't find anyone um maybe i would make  maybe i'll make an appeal here on my  channel who in prague is willing to  spend some time with me maybe i have to  pay for private lessons maybe i have to  go to school but i have to be submerged  immersed in check for five days okay so  i have to prepare for it and i have to  make sure that the conditions are there  that are going to enable me to do it now  the third thing of course is the  follow-up  because the five days to fluency  it doesn't mean that on day five you're  fluent  you'll make a major breakthrough a major  step forward but i have always found  that in language learning there is this  gestation period it's it's like baking  bread in the oven  so  you make a major step forward during  these five days  but you will find that the  impact of these five days will continue  for quite a while so after you return  then  you will continue maintenance activities  reading and listening and occasionally  speaking and you'll find that that you  will continue to improve in your degree  of fluency  so i mean obviously if you have three  months to spend instead of five days  you'll do even better but i i like the  idea that you focus on this five-day  total immersion  you make  all the preparations necessary in terms  of your language study beforehand in  terms of the circumstances that will  prevail while you're there so that you  do get that solid immersion and then the  maintenance work that you do  after the fact so there you have it that  is my five days to fluency  program  uh and check will be the first test of  this  uh i've been at check now for about  eight months off and on  between you know leaving it to travel to  australia doing a bit of russian here  and there but  i'm on my check and i'm gonna crank it  up a bit although i am interspersing it  with russian i think as i get closer to  october i might stop doing my russian  focus on the check the objective being  that when i hit prague for five days i'm  going to speak nothing but check and as  a result i will convert myself from  someone who stumbles in the language  understands it quite well but stumbles  when speaking into someone  who maybe not on the fifth day but  soon thereafter will be  able to say  i am fluent in check we'll see what  happens and i look forward to your  feedback bye for now

